            3. The Science of Subtractive Mixing: Kubelka-Munk vs Additive RGB
          </h3>
          <p>
            Understanding why an <strong>online color mixer</strong> needs physical science requires distinguishing between how monitors display light and how pigments reflect light:
          </p>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
            <div class="rounded-xl border border-[#ebebeb] dark:border-[#222222] bg-[#f9fafb] dark:bg-[#121212] p-5">
              <div class="font-mono text-xs font-semibold text-[#0070f3] uppercase">Additive RGB (Digital Displays)</div>
              <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-[#4d4d4d] dark:text-[#a1a1aa] leading-relaxed">
                Computer monitors emit light. Blending red, green, and blue light adds wavelengths together toward white. Mixing complementary screen colors produces grey because opposite wavelengths cancel each other out in the eye's photoreceptors.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl border border-[#ebebeb] dark:border-[#222222] bg-[#f9fafb] dark:bg-[#121212] p-5">
              <div class="font-mono text-xs font-semibold text-[#168a35] dark:text-[#47d169] uppercase">Subtractive Pigments (Kubelka-Munk)</div>
              <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-[#4d4d4d] dark:text-[#a1a1aa] leading-relaxed">
                Physical pigments absorb (subtract) light. Blue paint absorbs red and yellow light; yellow paint absorbs blue and violet light. The only light reflected by both pigments is <strong>vibrant green</strong>.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            Our <strong>color mixer generator</strong> computes this reaction using the Kubelka-Munk equation:
          </p>
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-[#e5e7eb] dark:border-[#262626] bg-[#f9fafb] dark:bg-[#121212] p-4 font-mono text-xs text-[#111827] dark:text-[#ededed] text-center">
            K / S = (1 - R)² / (2R)
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-[#4d4d4d] dark:text-[#a1a1aa]">
            Where <em>K</em> is the absorption coefficient, <em>S</em> is the scattering coefficient, and <em>R</em> is the spectral reflectance at each visible wavelength.
          </p>
        </section>

